WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.520
For almost four years.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.680
American soldiers and Marines
in the Pacific

3
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.320
have struggled across beaches.
through jungles

4
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:16.840
and atop mountains to subdue
a nation that refuses to surrender.

5
00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.400
The effort has been immense.

6
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.160
More is required.

7
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.560
In the spring of 1945.

8
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.320
the United States
will start the final push

9
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.040
to defeat the Japanese empire.

10
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.240
All wars changed the world.

11
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.600
but none of them changed the world
like the Second World War did.

12
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.800
Japan's on the march.
Germany's on the march.

13
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.840
No one can imagine the nightmare
they're about to unleash.

14
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.560
The most destructive war
in human history.

15
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.200
Suddenly the world
is turned upside down

16
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.800
and all hell is let loose.

17
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.840
The West is stunned by
the speed of the advance.

18
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:01.040
You get the allies
led by the big three:

19
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.560
Roosevelt. Churchill. Stalin.

20
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.400
Men who are dealing with immensely
complicated questions.

21
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:12.080
It's the biggest military
operation of human history.

22
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.440
The allies have to come together.
not just militarily.

23
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.400
but industrial scale.
It's a global perspective.

24
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.560
They have to fight
in every climate.

25
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.080
from the Arctic
to the jungles of the Pacific.

26
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.840
to the deserts of Africa
and the depths of the ocean.

27
00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.800
But there was no
certainty of victory.

28
00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.360
It was going to be
a horrific bloodbath.

29
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.880
We see humans
at their absolute worst.

30
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.200
how they treat other human beings.

31
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.200
and we see them
at their absolute best.

32
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.560
willing to give their lives
that others might live.

33
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.360
World War II was a struggle
in which there could be one victor

34
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:50.480
and vanquished.

35
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.240
With the Rhine's west bank secured.

36
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.880
Britain expects other crossings
of Germany's river of destiny.

37
00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.640
after a law to build up
our own strength.

38
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:23.280
and after that. the end of the
European war should not be far off.

39
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.160
In America
in the spring of 1945.

40
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:32.640
there is hope that the war
in Europe is nearing its end.

41
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.440
Husbands and fathers.

42
00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:36.240
boyfriends and brothers

43
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.280
might finally be coming home.

44
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.600
It is front and centre
on the American mind.

45
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.480
How quickly can we get to Berlin

46
00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.840
and get the soldiers home?

47
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.560
The public get this sort of
an attitude that. you know.

48
00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:53.760
hey. the war's almost over.

49
00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.120
They'll be coming home.
but it's almost like a marathon

50
00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.800
and you see the finish line
in the distance.

51
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.760
and so you know you can give one
last kick and then somebody says.

52
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.480
"Actually there's another
three miles to go."

53
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.280
Victory in Europe
seems within reach.

54
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.480
Victory over Tokyo
remains on the far horizon.

55
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.200
For months. hundreds of B-29s
have been striking Japanese cities

56
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:24.840
to marginal effect.

57
00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.560
The army air forces believed

58
00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.640
that a sufficient amount of
pinpoint bombing from high altitude

59
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.800
would take out Japanese industry

60
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.360
and make it impossible for them
to continue the war.

61
00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:38.680
It didn't work.

62
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.360
Bombing from 30.000 feet.
the winds were too strong.

63
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.160
blew the bombs all over the place.

64
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.200
Under the command
of General Curtis LeMay.

65
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:52.040
the Air Force alters its tactics.

66
00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.000
On March 9th. 1945.

67
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:10.160
LeMay's B-29s will attack Tokyo from
only 5.000 feet using a new weapon.

68
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.880
Bombs filled with napalm.

69
00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.600
Napalm is this gel combined with
gasoline that will stick to things

70
00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:21.880
and then it catches fire and it
makes it very. very hard to put out.

71
00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:25.440
It's for starting fires that are
designed to just go out of control.

72
00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.480
And the reason it's gonna be
so destructive is that Tokyo is

73
00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.120
mostly made of wooden paper
and this is just going to explode.

74
00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.560
It'll be incredibly inflammable.

75
00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.280
The argument was that

76
00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.720
if you destroy the housing units
of the Japanese workers.

77
00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.200
they wouldn't be able to go to work
and build the planes and tanks.

78
00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:45.960
They needed to continue
the conflict.

79
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:51.720
But the outcome
was absolutely horrific.

80
00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.080
It is just complete
devastation in Tokyo.

81
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.800
100.000 people were killed.

82
00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.640
We think of Dresden as
being the worst single moment

83
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.560
in the European Theatre
of Operations.

84
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.160
The fire storm at Dresden.

85
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.160
25.000 were lost there.

86
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.320
but in Tokyo

87
00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:24.560
there's a 100.000 killed
in a single operation.

88
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:27.760
The whole city was a sea of fire.

89
00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:32.560
It was so hot
that canals start to boil

90
00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:35.720
and the fires eat up so much of
the oxygen that a lot of the people

91
00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:39.040
who die asphyxiate
because there's no oxygen left.

92
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.200
A huge proportion of the city
is simply gone in one night.

93
00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:45.440
The destruction is immense.

94
00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.440
16 square miles of Tokyo
are obliterated.

95
00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:54.960
After this one raid. a million
Japanese no longer have a home.

96
00:05:56.280 --> 00:05:57.720
It's unprecedented.

97
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.520
General LeMay later I recall that

98
00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.640
he would've been tried
as a war criminal

99
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:10.440
if United States lost in the war.

100
00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.000
To survey the damage.

101
00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:22.920
Emperor Hirohito emerges from his
bunker beneath the Imperial Palace.

102
00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:29.680
Emperor Hirohito has been pretty
isolated throughout the war.

103
00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.680
but he does have a sense
that the war is not exactly

104
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.120
going as his generals
and admirals are telling him.

105
00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.960
So when the fire bombing
in Tokyo happened.

106
00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.360
that only intensifies his desire

107
00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:44.800
to start looking for ways
to end the war.

108
00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.040
For Emperor Hirohito.
the big concern is not what might

109
00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.560
happen to the ordinary
Japanese people.

110
00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.240
but if the Americans
make it onto Japanese soil.

111
00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.840
what might happen to him?

112
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.840
What might happen
to the imperial line

113
00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:00.280
that goes back centuries in Japan?

114
00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:03.920
He says.
"I can't possibly face my ancestors

115
00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:05.840
having sacrificed the throne."

116
00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.960
The emperor of Japan
is considered to be divine

117
00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:13.120
with immense authority
over his people.

118
00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.800
But Hirohito rarely
exerts that authority

119
00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.160
in political or military matters.

120
00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.000
There's the committee for the
supreme direction of the war.

121
00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.480
which is six people.
two people from the army.

122
00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:27.040
two people from the navy. the prime
minister and the foreign minister.

123
00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:30.720
Those six are the only six that
had any real say within Japan.

124
00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.400
The civilian leaders understood
they couldn't win the war.

125
00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.920
but the military
are determined to fight on.

126
00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:40.560
Japanese military leaders
have lived their whole life

127
00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.200
under a professional code.

128
00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.200
That surrender is so dishonourable
you would rather die.

129
00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:50.520
no matter what the suffering.
the warrior does not surrender.

130
00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.040
For all the firebombing.
for all the destruction

131
00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.520
already rained on Japan.
there's no sign of surrender.

132
00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:00.960
The American high command
now believes that the Japanese

133
00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.320
would not surrender
short of an invasion

134
00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.000
and occupation of the Japanese
home islands.

135
00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:12.280
The United States is gathering
its forces for the next phase

136
00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.760
of its assault on Imperial Japan.

137
00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.400
The capture of Okinawa.

138
00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:24.560
the largest island
on the Ryukyu chain.

139
00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.440
Okinawa is just 400 miles
south of Kyushu.

140
00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:30.960
which is the most southerly
of the Japanese home islands.

141
00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:34.080
and therefore can be used
to launch an invasion fleet.

142
00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:38.240
So Okinawa is absolutely
vital to the Americans.

143
00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:46.760
Okinawa would be defended

144
00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:50.080
by about 100.000
Japanese frontline troops

145
00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.360
and also native Okinawan
militia forces.

146
00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.720
They know that the Americans
have overwhelming superiority

147
00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:00.240
in the air and at sea.

148
00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:05.400
The plan for the defence
of Okinawa

149
00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:06.840
is don't go for a Banzai charge.

150
00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:10.280
Instead. they will prepare
these defensive fortifications.

151
00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.040
let the Americans come to them
and take them out that way.

152
00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.720
Using native
Okinawans as forced labour.

153
00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:23.960
the Japanese build a network
of bunkers and tunnels.

154
00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.280
And the idea
was kill four. five Americans

155
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:28.760
before you are killed yourself.

156
00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.480
The Japanese overall strategy
is to make the Americans pay

157
00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.240
so much for Okinawa that an invasion
of the Japanese home islands

158
00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.080
would be unthinkable.

159
00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:47.120
The garrison on Okinawa
is only half the Japanese strategy

160
00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.680
for defending the island.

161
00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:51.440
The other element of the plan
is to use kamikaze.

162
00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.160
but in far greater numbers
than ever been used before.

163
00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:58.080
The Japanese recognise that
when the Americans conduct

164
00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:01.200
these amphibious landing operations.
the ships offshore are the lifeline

165
00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:04.760
that's keeping the battalions ashore
in the fight.

166
00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:09.200
So they came up with this idea
of these organised waves

167
00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:10.640
of kamikaze attacks.

168
00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:16.920
And if you launch 300
kamikaze at the American fleet

169
00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:20.040
and you take out seven aircraft
carriers or something.

170
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.760
that is a war-changing thing.

171
00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.480
The Battle of Okinawa
starts with the largest invasion

172
00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:34.320
of the war in the Pacific.

173
00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.720
A massive effort combining
naval. air and ground support.

174
00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:44.320
60.000 soldiers and marines are
bound for Okinawa's western beaches.

175
00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.120
The invasion force
will land on Hagushi Bay.

176
00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:51.240
then the Marines will sweep north.
and the army south.

177
00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.640
to take control of
the entire island.

178
00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.200
The biggest danger
in an amphibious operation

179
00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:04.360
is the actual landing because
you are incredibly vulnerable.

180
00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:06.200
You are out in the open.

181
00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:08.000
The combat troops going up first.

182
00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:12.640
Both Marine and Army have been
told to expect up to 80% casualties.

183
00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:18.360
So if you've drawn the unlucky straw
of being one of the first units.

184
00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:21.400
you're pretty convinced not
many of you're gonna survive.

185
00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:25.320
They get to the beaches
start walking up it.

186
00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:28.880
body's tense waiting
for the bullets to come...

187
00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.440
...and there is nothing.

188
00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:36.960
They're experiencing
virtually no opposition.

189
00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:38.640
And so there was a thought that

190
00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:43.040
hey. maybe Okinawa's not gonna turn
out to be a big bloodbath after all.

191
00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.560
The first few days go
very well for the landing forces.

192
00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:50.600
They cut across all the way
to the other side of the island

193
00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:52.880
against minimal opposition.

194
00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.840
It's looking too good
to be true at this stage.

195
00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.600
Military leaders believed
that Japan must fight to protect

196
00:12:06.760 --> 00:12:09.160
the honour of the country.

197
00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:13.920
And these kamikaze pilots
are the chosen ones

198
00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:17.680
to go to this divine mission
to protect their homeland.

199
00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:22.400
They really believe that that
would turn the tide of the war.

200
00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:27.200
The pilots are young.
many of them are teenagers.

201
00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:28.880
they're ill-trained.

202
00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:31.440
They don't even have enough
fuel to come back from this flight

203
00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.400
if they wanted to.
They know they're going to die.

204
00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:41.200
So when they get to the first
American ship they see.

205
00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:44.000
they attack.

206
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:01.000
For the first five days
of the invasion of Okinawa.

207
00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:05.440
there's no opposition to the massive
US fleet surrounding the island.

208
00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:13.240
But just after noon on April 6th.
the Japanese arrive in force.

209
00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:21.840
Imagine we're on the bridge
of one of these destroyers

210
00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:23.520
80 miles north of Okinawa.

211
00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.320
The kamikaze come from starboard.
from port. from high. from low.

212
00:13:33.240 --> 00:13:36.440
So you feel like you're in a boxing
ring with about five other boxers

213
00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.280
attacking you from every direction.

214
00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:49.480
Hundreds of kamikaze at a time
thrown against the invading fleet.

215
00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:51.400
They thought with the sheer numbers.

216
00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:53.320
they could take out
the capital ships;

217
00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:55.400
that's battleships
and aircraft carriers

218
00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:58.000
and drive off the US fleet
in its entirety.

219
00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:03.200
The kamikaze don't
really have much success

220
00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.320
against aircraft carriers...

221
00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:14.080
...but 300+ ships
are targeted by the kamikazes.

222
00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:16.680
More than 36 of them sink.

223
00:14:19.120 --> 00:14:22.840
It is more devastating than
any conventional bombing attack

224
00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:27.560
has been in the war so far.
but it's not enough.

225
00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:32.960
What the kamikaze tells me is that
the Japanese knew the war was lost.

226
00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:34.960
at least by conventional means.

227
00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:40.240
There's nothing more dangerous
in warfare than a cornered enemy.

228
00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.680
The Japanese
are gonna go down in flames

229
00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:45.520
but will never surrender. never.

230
00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:47.840
For the US Navy.

231
00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:50.000
the battle against the kamikaze
at Okinawa

232
00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:53.160
is bloodier than any
previous confrontation

233
00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:55.720
of the entire Pacific War.

234
00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.760
And American ground forces
are now facing

235
00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.040
increasing resistance as well.

236
00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:06.720
The force that had landed on
the beaches is pushing south toward

237
00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:08.760
a place called Kakazu Ridge

238
00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.800
where they encounter
the first line of Japanese defences.

239
00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:18.720
It's the first time the
Americans meet serious opposition

240
00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:20.320
and it stops them dead.

241
00:15:26.400 --> 00:15:28.760
The Japanese have created
a defensive system that goes

242
00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:31.240
right across the whole
width of the island.

243
00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:33.400
so they can't be out-flanked.

244
00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:37.720
American troops endure
days of non-stop enemy fire.

245
00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:45.080
This is attritional.
grizzly. meat grinding fighting.

246
00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:48.600
and that's just the first line
of defence.

247
00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:51.160
There are gonna be
many more beyond this.

248
00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:54.880
What we thought was gonna
be a cakewalk is anything but.

249
00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:00.320
April 12th.

250
00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:04.120
The American people learn
Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

251
00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:07.720
the man they elected four times
is dead.

252
00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:15.200
Vice president Harry S. Truman.
a former judge and senator

253
00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:17.960
from Missouri
is sworn in hours later.

254
00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:22.280
Roosevelt had led the country
through the Great Depression

255
00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:24.080
and through the war.

256
00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:28.480
In the 82 days that Truman
had been vice president.

257
00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:31.760
he met with Roosevelt all of twice.

258
00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:35.440
He really knows nothing about
what's going on with the war effort.

259
00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:37.120
He didn't think
he was up to the job

260
00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:39.680
and there are people
who would say he was right.

261
00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:42.280
The new president
is immediately confronted

262
00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:44.120
with enormous challenges.

263
00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:48.040
It's important for the wartime
president to demonstrate

264
00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:51.080
that he has a plan for bringing
this war to a successful conclusion

265
00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.200
and as rapidly as possible.

266
00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:55.800
It's clear to the American
population that Germany

267
00:16:55.960 --> 00:16:59.440
is on its last legs.
but Japan is still fighting hard.

268
00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:01.040
The question really is.

269
00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:04.440
how many American lives am
I going to lose to end this war?

270
00:17:04.600 --> 00:17:07.560
That's the whole ball game
for Harry Truman.

271
00:17:13.480 --> 00:17:16.360
Truman meets with
Secretary of War Henry Stimson

272
00:17:16.520 --> 00:17:18.920
and Army General Leslie Groves.

273
00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:21.360
The agenda is classified.

274
00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:23.920
They hand Truman a document.

275
00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:25.640
Truman opens it up and reads it

276
00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:28.120
and the memo begins
with these dramatic words.

277
00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:32.360
"Within four months. we shall.
in all probability. have completed

278
00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:35.840
the most terrible weapon
ever known in human history.

279
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:40.160
one bomb which can
destroy a whole city."

280
00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:45.640
The Manhattan Project.
the top secret American program

281
00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:48.400
to develop the world's
first atomic bombs.

282
00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:53.960
For three years. thousands
of scientists and engineers

283
00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.520
have worked on the most powerful
weapon in history.

284
00:17:57.680 --> 00:18:00.120
It is set to be tested mid-summer.

285
00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:03.880
Truman and his commanders
are ready to pay any price

286
00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.080
to bring this war to an end.

287
00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.720
But an invasion of
the Japanese home islands

288
00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:10.880
will be bloody
on an unimaginable scale.

289
00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:13.680
Higher losses than
any previous campaign.

290
00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:16.720
either in the Pacific War
or in the war in Europe.

291
00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:23.400
The Manhattan project. the attempt
to develop atomic weapons.

292
00:18:23.560 --> 00:18:26.040
If that proves to be successful.

293
00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:30.440
if those tests prove out.
then that is an alternative.

294
00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:43.920
The war in Europe is over.

295
00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:46.160
The Nazi Third Reich
has been destroyed.

296
00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.440
But for war-weary America
and its allies.

297
00:18:56.440 --> 00:18:58.640
the war in the Pacific continues.

298
00:18:58.800 --> 00:19:01.640
and many of the men
who helped defeat Germany

299
00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:05.040
may now be required to fight Japan.

300
00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.360
At this point.
people in the military knew

301
00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:10.280
that a lot of these units are
gonna need to be transferred.

302
00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:13.520
Just because the war in Europe
is over. don't get excited.

303
00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:16.040
We're gonna need those troops
in the Pacific.

304
00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.320
After two months
of ceaseless combat.

305
00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:25.960
Marine and Army units break through

306
00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:29.080
the main Japanese defensive line
on Okinawa.

307
00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:32.760
Japanese troops
are forced to retreat

308
00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:36.640
and attempt a last stand
on the Kiyan Peninsula.

309
00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:39.320
By this point.
the Japanese soldiers on Okinawa

310
00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:40.680
are low on ammunition.

311
00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:42.840
they are starving.
they're exhausted.

312
00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:46.520
but they realise that the next step
for the allies after Okinawa

313
00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:48.000
is the home islands.

314
00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:51.240
They are all that stand between
the allies and their families.

315
00:19:51.400 --> 00:19:52.640
their homes.

316
00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:56.840
They are the last line of defence
and everyone is depending on them.

317
00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:05.760
Although the Americans
have gained ground...

318
00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:11.320
...the more you push and the more
terrain that has been captured.

319
00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:14.200
the more dangerous and costly
the battle is becoming.

320
00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:18.480
But the Americans know
we're making progress.

321
00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:20.400
If war is a clash of wills.

322
00:20:20.560 --> 00:20:23.400
the United States is a nation
willing to fight it out to the end.

323
00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:30.680
Organised resistance on Okinawa
ends On June 22nd.

324
00:20:31.840 --> 00:20:34.000
The Japanese army's
commanding general

325
00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:38.880
and his chief of staff both
commit suicide by ritual seppuku.

326
00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:42.280
100.000 Japanese
servicemen are killed.

327
00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:47.320
On the American side. the casualties
are 50.000 killed and wounded.

328
00:20:48.200 --> 00:20:51.840
These are the worst of battle
casualties in the Pacific War

329
00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.000
for the Americans.

330
00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:58.040
worse than all of that are
the number of civilians killed.

331
00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:00.280
Up to 125.000.

332
00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:04.160
which is a third of the population
caught in the crossfire.

333
00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:06.040
This is just Okinawa.

334
00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:08.400
What happens when we
get to their home islands?

335
00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:11.360
The cost is gonna be enormous.

336
00:21:11.520 --> 00:21:15.440
This war ain't over.
It's not even close to being over.

337
00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:23.280
American leaders understand
the Battle of Okinawa

338
00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.920
is a frightening preview
of what to expect

339
00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:28.880
when Japan's home islands
are invaded.

340
00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:30.960
Truman has a meeting
with General Marshall

341
00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:34.000
and the joint chiefs of staff
on Operation Downfall;

342
00:21:34.160 --> 00:21:36.880
the invasion of the Japanese
home islands.

343
00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:39.720
This plan goes back to
the Roosevelt presidency.

344
00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:41.360
Planning is going forward

345
00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:45.080
and the most interested person
at the meeting is George Marshall.

346
00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:48.040
He wants Truman to sign off on it.

347
00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:50.800
Operation Downfall will be
a two-pronged operation.

348
00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:52.920
the first called Operation Olympic.

349
00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.480
The idea of Operation Olympic is to
invade the southern island of Kyushu

350
00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:02.400
and seize naval bases and air bases
while pushing Japanese forces north.

351
00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:04.760
The critical phase. though.
of Operation Downfall

352
00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:07.000
is what's code-named
Operation Coronet.

353
00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:10.040
It's the invasion of Honshu.
which is where Tokyo itself sits.

354
00:22:10.200 --> 00:22:11.600
So this would inevitably end

355
00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:15.000
in either the destruction
or capture of the Japanese capital.

356
00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:19.280
Think about the scale of
Operation Downfall as a whole.

357
00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:20.760
5 million men involved.

358
00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:23.280
up to a million British
and Commonwealth troops.

359
00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:26.880
4 million American troops.
and this is gonna dwarf D-Day

360
00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:30.560
in the logistics and the naval of
forces that are gonna be involved.

361
00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:33.120
Then the forces that
are actually gonna land

362
00:22:33.280 --> 00:22:36.400
and most importantly of all is gonna
dwarf the number of casualties.

363
00:22:38.280 --> 00:22:40.560
If the allies
had to invade Japan.

364
00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:44.120
that would be the largest amphibious
assault ever undertaken.

365
00:22:45.160 --> 00:22:48.920
And the Americans were gonna
bear almost the entire weight of it.

366
00:22:50.000 --> 00:22:51.560
and the President understood that

367
00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:53.880
better than most of
the American public did.

368
00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:57.960
Truman specifically
says to his chiefs of staff.

369
00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:01.080
I don't want an Okinawa from
one end of Japan to the other.

370
00:23:05.520 --> 00:23:08.480
So Truman. in June of '45.

371
00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:12.760
is looking at a brutal final act
against Japan.

372
00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:14.920
But it is very important
to Harry Truman

373
00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:18.160
that he always be
seen as the decider.

374
00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:19.920
The buck stops here.

375
00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:24.920
Truman understands that this
war with Japan needs to end.

376
00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:27.120
the invasion looks horrible.

377
00:23:27.280 --> 00:23:30.480
But what's option B
in this situation?

378
00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:32.360
Even at this late date in June.

379
00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:35.960
there's no sense that the Manhattan
Project is going to work.

380
00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.320
They really don't know
if they have a bomb yet.

381
00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:41.440
And so Truman signs off
on Operation Downfall.

382
00:23:41.600 --> 00:23:44.160
the two big invasions of Japan.

383
00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:48.560
Stage one. Operation Olympic
is set for November 1st.

384
00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:50.160
The clock is ticking.

385
00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:59.200
The Japanese army's plan
is to fight to the bitter end

386
00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:04.120
with a decisive and final battle
in the home islands.

387
00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:07.720
For a while now.
the Japanese army had been planning

388
00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:10.720
what they call Operation Ketsugo.
which means operation decisive.

389
00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.600
It's a plan to defend Kyushu.
They think they've got

390
00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:16.520
perhaps a million men that
they can put into the field.

391
00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:20.920
They also want to put civilians
into the field.

392
00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:24.240
Their idea is that they will
make things so terrifying.

393
00:24:24.400 --> 00:24:26.160
that it will stop the Americans

394
00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:28.760
trying to do anything anywhere else
in the home islands.

395
00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:33.360
Women and children were
going through the military drills

396
00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:35.840
where everybody was
given a bamboo spear.

397
00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:42.960
They are essentially mobilising
everybody older than 12 years old...

398
00:24:45.360 --> 00:24:47.080
...to fight to the death.

399
00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:02.040
In July. for the first time.
President Truman

400
00:25:02.200 --> 00:25:05.280
meets with both British Prime
Minister Winston Churchill

401
00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:08.920
and Soviet Premier Joseph Stalin
at Potsdam.

402
00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:12.560
formerly the home of Prussian kings
and the German Kaiser.

403
00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:15.560
Europe is devastated.

404
00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.360
It's a massive disaster zone.

405
00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:23.800
The point of the Potsdam Conference

406
00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:26.560
is for these three governments
to come together

407
00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:29.520
and figure out how these countries
are gonna be rebuilt.

408
00:25:30.720 --> 00:25:34.320
But Truman has his eyes on
a completely different problem.

409
00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:36.920
He huddles with
Secretary of War Stimson.

410
00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:38.880
and what are they talking about?

411
00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:40.960
They're talking about the Pacific.

412
00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:46.520
With Operation Downfall
months away.

413
00:25:46.680 --> 00:25:49.880
President Truman seeks
assurance that the Soviets

414
00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:53.760
will participate in the war
against Japan as pledged.

415
00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:57.840
All this time. Japan and
the Soviet Union had been neutral.

416
00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:00.000
They had not been at war
with one another.

417
00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:04.360
Stalin promised within 90 days
of the end of the war in Europe.

418
00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:06.240
he'll join the war against Japan.

419
00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:08.960
He promised that to Roosevelt
back at Yalta.

420
00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:10.960
So Truman hopes that's a go.

421
00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:14.000
But at the start
of the conference.

422
00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:17.680
Stimson receives a coded cable
from the War Department.

423
00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:22.680
"Operated on this morning.
diagnosis not yet complete.

424
00:26:22.840 --> 00:26:24.760
but results seem satisfactory.

425
00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:27.000
Dr Groves is pleased."

426
00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:32.640
Truman now knew that the atomic bomb
had been detonated successfully.

427
00:26:35.400 --> 00:26:36.520
In this moment.

428
00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:39.040
we have this amazing window
into Truman's thinking.

429
00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:40.720
because he was keeping a diary.

430
00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:46.680
Truman realises this bomb could be
a short circuit to ending the war.

431
00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:52.680
When Truman hears that
the atomic bomb actually works.

432
00:26:52.840 --> 00:26:54.800
he knows they have to use it.

433
00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.640
but he wants to give the Japanese

434
00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:59.320
one last chance to surrender
before he does.

435
00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:03.760
President Truman. along
with Churchill and Chiang Kai-shek.

436
00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:08.960
the leader of the Republic of China.
issue the Potsdam Declaration.

437
00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:10.920
a warning to Japan of what awaits

438
00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:13.320
if they don't surrender
unconditionally.

439
00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:15.440
But Stimson suggests

440
00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:19.560
they consider allowing Hirohito
to keep his throne.

441
00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:23.240
Stimson understands the
constitutional and cultural position

442
00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:27.640
that the emperor plays in Japanese
life. this kind of demigod status.

443
00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:30.960
So he insists on inserting
into the declaration.

444
00:27:31.120 --> 00:27:34.000
assurance that they won't try
the emperor for war crimes.

445
00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:37.080
But beyond that. it will be
unconditional surrender.

446
00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:39.120
and no other terms
will be acceptable.

447
00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:42.920
Also in this document is wordage

448
00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:45.240
that does not mention
the atomic bomb.

449
00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:48.640
but it does say that if
unconditional surrender

450
00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:53.960
is not coming. the destruction was
going to be historic and massive.

451
00:27:58.360 --> 00:28:02.560
The Office of War Information
releases the Potsdam Declaration

452
00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:06.800
and the Japanese receive it
via radio from San Francisco.

453
00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:12.280
Prime Minister Suzuki
and Emperor Hirohito

454
00:28:12.440 --> 00:28:15.280
are of the opinion that

455
00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:21.440
the Potsdam Declaration
is not really vindictive.

456
00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:26.080
They see some hope.

457
00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:29.440
Emperor Hirohito himself.
at this point.

458
00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.320
he is open to accepting it. but he

459
00:28:32.480 --> 00:28:35.800
understands his role as an emperor.

460
00:28:35.960 --> 00:28:39.400
There are only certain times
for him to weigh in.

461
00:28:39.560 --> 00:28:41.240
The emperor
and the Peace Party

462
00:28:41.400 --> 00:28:43.040
are in an impossible position here.

463
00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:46.000
I mean. if they respond
positively to the ultimatum.

464
00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:47.600
the military might
turn against them.

465
00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:49.120
but if they completely ignore it.

466
00:28:49.280 --> 00:28:51.760
there's a danger that the Americans
are actually gonna carry out

467
00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:53.280
what they suggest.

468
00:28:54.480 --> 00:28:55.760
Two days later.

469
00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:59.880
Japan's Prime Minister Kantaro
Suzuki holds a press conference.

470
00:29:01.280 --> 00:29:04.680
When press asks
about Potsdam Declaration.

471
00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:07.440
Suzuki uses the word "mokusatsu."

472
00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:11.760
The best translation for that
is "no comment"

473
00:29:11.920 --> 00:29:15.400
or I'm not going to respond
to that question.

474
00:29:16.360 --> 00:29:20.920
However. the word mokusatsu can
also mean we're going to ignore it.

475
00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:24.000
I think the Japanese media.
they're very quick to interpret it

476
00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:25.920
in the most hostile
and negative way.

477
00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:28.760
Then the American media pick it up.

478
00:29:28.920 --> 00:29:32.440
then that becomes the view of the
American administration as well

479
00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:36.320
that the Japanese are rejecting
the Potsdam Declaration.

480
00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:39.920
And to Truman. the Japanese
response is plain and clear.

481
00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:43.840
He believes that the Japanese
told him to go to hell.

482
00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:57.600
By early 1945.

483
00:29:57.760 --> 00:30:02.360
Tinian Island in the Marianas
is the largest air base on Earth.

484
00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:06.960
Hundreds of B-29s have taken off

485
00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:09.600
from its multiple runways
to bomb Japan.

486
00:30:16.200 --> 00:30:19.800
But there's a very mysterious
restricted area

487
00:30:19.960 --> 00:30:21.720
at one part of the base.

488
00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:24.600
No one really knows
what's going on up there.

489
00:30:24.760 --> 00:30:29.120
It belongs to some unit called
the 509th Composite Group.

490
00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:33.480
These are the preparations
for the arrival of the atomic bomb.

491
00:30:35.360 --> 00:30:39.280
Components for the atomic
bombs are delivered in late July

492
00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.720
and carefully assembled
over the following days.

493
00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:48.040
So the Manhattan Project was
able to produce enough uranium

494
00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:49.960
and plutonium for three bombs.

495
00:30:50.120 --> 00:30:52.200
And after the Trinity test.

496
00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:55.080
the Americans only had
two bombs in the arsenal.

497
00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:58.200
Truman makes
the decision to drop the bomb.

498
00:30:58.360 --> 00:31:00.560
He legitimately wants
to save US casualties

499
00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:03.120
and bring this war
to an end rapidly.

500
00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:07.200
At the same time. he wants to assert
his authority as president.

501
00:31:13.560 --> 00:31:16.680
It is one of the most
consequential decisions that...

502
00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:21.120
...any nation has ever made.

503
00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:27.120
and no one knows
what was gonna happen.

504
00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:30.680
But President Truman did understand

505
00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:33.600
that this was of a different
scale and scope.

506
00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:37.480
He compares it to the biblical flood
in his diary.

507
00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:41.880
"As the world was once destroyed
by water for Noah.

508
00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:45.240
perhaps now it'll be destroyed
by fire and destruction."

509
00:31:49.760 --> 00:31:53.080
By design. the target
of the first atomic bomb is a city

510
00:31:53.240 --> 00:31:55.520
relatively unscathed by the war.

511
00:31:56.600 --> 00:32:01.240
American military leaders
want the Japanese to understand

512
00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:04.960
the awful destructive power
of this new weapon.

513
00:32:05.120 --> 00:32:08.160
That morning in Hiroshima
is an ordinary morning.

514
00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:09.760
People are going
about their business.

515
00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:12.000
Some people are going
into the factories to work.

516
00:32:12.160 --> 00:32:14.520
They're still wondering why
they've not yet been bombed

517
00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.280
and they're expecting it
more or less any day.

518
00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:20.280
And then people see
this light in the sky.

519
00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:24.120
sun shining off a single aircraft.

520
00:32:56.440 --> 00:33:00.280
The first thing is
the flashing light

521
00:33:00.440 --> 00:33:02.520
as if sun explodes.

522
00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:06.240
then a noise and a shock.

523
00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:15.360
The people who were near
the epicentre evaporate.

524
00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:19.560
The casualties mount
until you get between 90.000

525
00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:22.240
and 160.000 people who've died.

526
00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:27.520
The dominant image that
survivors give of Hiroshima

527
00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:29.920
is of one of the Buddhist hells.

528
00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:38.040
One thing really difficult
for historians to jive with

529
00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:41.720
is this idea that Truman
has chosen military targets

530
00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:45.160
and he writes even in his diary. the
words. "And not women and children."

531
00:33:46.360 --> 00:33:48.440
We'll never really know.

532
00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:51.760
Did he really feel that
this was a military target

533
00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:55.120
or was he writing that in his diary
understanding that

534
00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:59.520
history for generations
was gonna judge him for this?

535
00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:05.600
Truman is at sea
coming home from Potsdam

536
00:34:05.760 --> 00:34:07.920
when he received the news.

537
00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:10.640
Later that same day.
he goes on the airwaves to address

538
00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.480
the American people and to tell them
what has just happened.

539
00:34:13.640 --> 00:34:17.600
He says it's a new type of bomb
that America has used it

540
00:34:17.760 --> 00:34:20.640
and will continue to use it
until Japan surrenders.

541
00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:23.760
If they do not now
accept our terms.

542
00:34:23.920 --> 00:34:26.720
they may expect a rain of ruin
from the air.

543
00:34:26.880 --> 00:34:29.840
the like of which has never
been seen on this earth.

544
00:34:36.160 --> 00:34:41.200
Even with Hiroshima in ruins.
Japanese leaders do not surrender.

545
00:34:41.360 --> 00:34:44.240
The peace camp
pushed for immediate acceptance

546
00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:46.720
of the Potsdam Declaration.

547
00:34:46.880 --> 00:34:50.560
but the military side insists
on continuing the war.

548
00:34:50.720 --> 00:34:55.400
The reasoning is that may be
the only atomic bomb that they had.

549
00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:57.600
So they're still at the stalemate.

550
00:34:59.120 --> 00:35:01.800
And then the Soviet Union
declares war on Japan.

551
00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:10.160
Just as Stalin promised.

552
00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:13.800
exactly 90 days after
the surrender of Germany.

553
00:35:15.520 --> 00:35:18.200
one and a half million Soviet troops

554
00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:22.880
pour across the border into
Japanese occupied Manchuria.

555
00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:25.520
The Japanese aren't
aware that for months the Soviets

556
00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:29.200
have amassed their armour
on the borders with Manchuria.

557
00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:32.200
But now the Russians
are rolling into Manchuria

558
00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:34.720
and they're rolling over
the Japanese.

559
00:35:34.880 --> 00:35:37.080
And now you've got the prospect
of the Soviets.

560
00:35:37.240 --> 00:35:39.160
not just making their way
into Manchuria.

561
00:35:39.320 --> 00:35:41.600
but possibly across to the Japanese
home islands.

562
00:35:42.760 --> 00:35:45.440
For Japan. it's a complete disaster.

563
00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:52.560
On the same day the Red Army
moves into Manchuria.

564
00:35:55.280 --> 00:35:58.160
B-29s appear over Nagasaki.

565
00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:01.840
One carries an atomic bomb.

566
00:36:03.200 --> 00:36:05.920
The US has
just one more atom bomb left.

567
00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:10.080
But the whole idea is to give Japan
the impression

568
00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:12.400
that the US has an unlimited stock

569
00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:16.280
and that if Japan won't surrender.

570
00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:20.520
the US will continue to unleash
atom bombs on Japan.

571
00:36:24.560 --> 00:36:28.440
Between 60 and 70.000 people
are killed instantly.

572
00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:42.840
Prime Minister Suzuki
convenes the war council

573
00:36:43.000 --> 00:36:47.760
and the discussion is. are we gonna
accept the Potsdam Declaration?

574
00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:50.680
And the answer
from the military is no.

575
00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:55.560
You've had two atomic bombs.

576
00:36:55.720 --> 00:36:58.080
You've had the Soviet
invasion of Manchuria.

577
00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:01.080
and yet the Supreme War Council
is still split.

578
00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:03.040
The war faction. the military.

579
00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:06.320
they're absolutely not willing to
consider unconditional surrender.

580
00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:11.840
That's when Prime Minister
Suzuki makes the very important move

581
00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:16.200
saying that we need
to ask the emperor.

582
00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:18.840
This is a standout moment.

583
00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:20.920
This is something you
would never normally do.

584
00:37:21.080 --> 00:37:23.280
There's a sense Hirohito
is semi-divine

585
00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:26.520
and shouldn't be troubled with
the decision making process.

586
00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:28.880
It's a really extraordinary step.

587
00:37:29.960 --> 00:37:32.880
But Hirohito. I think.
sees the gravity of the situation.

588
00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:35.880
He sees that this is a moment
where it's his responsibility

589
00:37:36.040 --> 00:37:38.000
to step up as the leader
effectively.

590
00:37:39.480 --> 00:37:41.960
And he says.

591
00:37:42.120 --> 00:37:46.960
"We must bear the unbearable
and suffer the insufferable.

592
00:37:47.120 --> 00:37:51.200
We will end the war
and then move on."

593
00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:04.240
I have received this afternoon

594
00:38:04.400 --> 00:38:06.960
a message from the Japanese
government.

595
00:38:07.120 --> 00:38:11.960
I deem this reply. a full acceptance
of the Potsdam Declaration.

596
00:38:13.080 --> 00:38:16.520
which specifies the unconditional
surrender of Japan.

597
00:38:18.000 --> 00:38:19.600
And people go nuts.

598
00:38:19.760 --> 00:38:23.080
Tens of thousands of people gather
at the gate of the White House.

599
00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:27.720
The world has been holding
its breath since the war began.

600
00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:33.640
Now it is over
and there is great celebration.

601
00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:37.960
One of the crucial factors
in ending this war is that you have

602
00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:40.800
Harry Truman. an accidental
president who had to be briefed

603
00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:43.320
on the wartime situation
when he enters the Oval Office.

604
00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:49.760
And Hirohito. who has never
really made political decisions.

605
00:38:49.920 --> 00:38:54.720
They assert themselves and bring
the war to an end in August of 1945.

606
00:38:57.120 --> 00:39:03.120
Batan. Guadalcanal.
Iwo Jima. Okinawa.

607
00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:06.880
once obscure points on a map.

608
00:39:07.040 --> 00:39:10.240
now each place has a new meaning.

609
00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:19.040
In Japan on August 15th.

610
00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:23.920
Emperor Hirohito addresses a nation
that has never surrendered.

611
00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:27.800
Most Japanese had never
heard the emperor's voice before.

612
00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:31.160
but the Jewel Voice Broadcast
as it became known.

613
00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:35.720
is essential to getting most
Japanese used to the idea

614
00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:38.360
of laying their arms
and admitting surrender.

615
00:39:53.720 --> 00:39:55.920
When the Japanese
hear that the war is over.

616
00:39:56.080 --> 00:39:58.200
there has to be a combination
of feelings.

617
00:39:58.360 --> 00:40:00.960
We sacrificed all these people.
all this money

618
00:40:01.120 --> 00:40:04.280
and these cities of Japan.
and we lost.

619
00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:07.760
Shock. sadness.

620
00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:14.680
but also this incredible sense of
grief that we let the emperor down.

621
00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:17.800
Our lack of effort let
the emperor down.

622
00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:22.200
The Japanese accepted
the idea of surrender.

623
00:40:22.360 --> 00:40:24.520
The allies accepted
the idea of Hirohito

624
00:40:24.680 --> 00:40:28.520
remaining on the Japanese throne.
and that was the end of the war.

625
00:40:29.600 --> 00:40:32.280
September 2nd. 1945.

626
00:40:32.440 --> 00:40:36.280
on the deck of the USS Missouri
anchored in Tokyo Bay.

627
00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:40.120
the Japanese sign the instrument
of surrender.

628
00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:42.280
the largest conflict
in world history

629
00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:45.120
closes with a signature
and a speech.

630
00:40:46.360 --> 00:40:48.480
Douglas MacArthur
marks the occasion.

631
00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:52.080
"It is my earnest hope
and indeed the hope of all mankind.

632
00:40:52.240 --> 00:40:53.840
that from this solemn occasion.

633
00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:57.800
a better world shall emerge out of
the blood and carnage of the past."

634
00:40:57.960 --> 00:40:59.960
Subtitles by Sky Access Services
